7.10.501.576 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n\"status\": \"answered\",\n\"answer\": \"Fats are digested in the small intestine, specifically in its proximal part called the duodenum. The process involves two main steps:\\n\\n1. **Emulsification**: Bile juice, which is secreted by the liver and stored in the gall bladder (though storage isn't explicitly detailed, secretion from liver is), enters the small intestine. This bile juice breaks down large fat globules into smaller, fine globules. This process is known as emulsification. It increases the surface area for enzymes to act upon.\\n\\n2. **Enzymatic Digestion**: The pancreas secretes pancreatic juice into the duodenum. This juice contains an enzyme called **lipase**. Lipase acts on the emulsified fats to break them down further into simpler molecules, specifically fatty acids and glycerol. \\n\\nAdditionally, the walls of the small intestine secrete intestinal juice, which continues the digestion process, ensuring fats are broken down into their smallest absorbable units.\",\n\"selected_evidence_ids\": [\n\"E1\",\n\"E2\"\n],\n\"citations\": [\n{\n\"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n\"pdf_page\": 24,\n\"textbook_page\": 15\n},\n{\n\"evidence_id\": \"E2\",\n\"pdf_page\": 25,\n\"textbook_page\": 16\n}\n],\n\"missing_information\": []\n}"}
